<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">St Ninian's Isle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">St Ninian's Isle is a small island off the west coast of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>, joined to it by a sand bar. In 1958 excavations at its ruined chapel uncovered the St Ninian's Isle Treasure, a hoard of 28 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> objects, including bowls and penannular brooches, buried around 800. It is the finest surviving collection of Pictish metalwork and is held by the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/st-ninians-isle.jpg" alt="St Ninian's Isle" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gettons · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Ninian%27s_Isle_and_horses.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>St Ninian's Isle is a tied island, linked to the south-western coast of the Mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> by the largest tombolo in the UK. The tombolo, known locally as an ayre, from the Old Norse for gravel bank, is 500 metres long. In summer it stands above sea level and walkers can cross it. In winter stronger waves strip sand from the beach, so that it is usually covered at high tide, and sometimes throughout the tidal cycle, until the sand returns the following spring. Depending on the definition used, the isle is therefore either an island or a peninsula, with an area of about 72 hectares.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The island's history is far older than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> graves have been found within the walls of its chapel, formerly beneath the floor. Like other places in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-isles">Northern Isles</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Hebrides</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faroe-islands">Faroes</a>, it may have connections with the Culdees or the papar. The ruins of a 12th-century chapel stand near the end of the tombolo, dedicated to Saint Ninian of Galloway, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>'s patron saint. Excavation also found the remains of an earlier, pre-Norse chapel, which may point to a Culdee presence.</p><p>The treasure was found on 4 July 1958 by Douglas Coutts, a local schoolboy helping visiting archaeologists led by Professor A. C. O'Dell of Aberdeen University. It lay under a cross-marked slab in the floor of the early church, in a wooden box. The pieces, not all of which were probably new when they were deposited, are believed to date from about 750 to 825; the hoard was suspected to have been buried to hide it from, or stolen in, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The last family to live on the island, that of Henry Leask, who was married twice and had 13 children, left it in 1796. The island belongs to the civil parish of Dunrossness on the South Mainland, and the nearest settlement is Bigton. Many seabirds visit it, puffins among them, and several species nest there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “St Ninian's Isle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The elite displayed its wealth in silver, including massive silver chains and fine penannular brooches; the hoard from <b>St Ninian’s Isle</b> in Shetland, buried around 800, shows the skill of Pictish metalworkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>St Ninian's Isle</b> Treasure (c. 750–825 CE) holds the best collection of Pictish forms.</blockquote><a href="/culture/picts">Picts · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
